<|im_start|>user
Problem: During the World Cup, there are \( n \) different Panini stickers to collect. Marco's friends are trying to complete their collection, but nobody has a full set of stickers yet! A pair of his friends are said to be wholesome if their combined collection has at least one of each sticker. Marco knows the contents of everyone's collections, and wants to take them all to a restaurant for his birthday. However, he doesn't want any wholesome pairs sitting at the same table.

(i) Show that Marco might need to reserve at least \( n \) different tables.

(ii) Show that \( n \) tables will always be enough for Marco to achieve his goal.

Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.<|im_end|>
<|im_start|>assistant
<think>

</think>



Completion:
We are given a situation involving the **World Cup** stickers, where each of the $ n $ different stickers is a unique item. Marco has $ n $ friends who are trying to collect these stickers, and each friend has a **collection of some stickers**, yet none of them has a **complete set** of all $ n $ stickers.

Marco wants to **assign his friends to tables** in such a way that **no two friends who are "wholesome" (i.e., their combined collection has at least one of each of the $ n $ stickers)** are seated at the **same table**. Marco also wants to **minimize the number of tables**, but he's **not allowed to have more than one wholesome pair** at the same table.

---

### **(i) Show that Marco might need to reserve at least $ n $ different tables.**

We analyze this in terms of **graph theory**, specifically by treating **friends as vertices** and **tables as sets**.

Each friend has a **collection of stickers**—let’s say the friend has a **subset of the $ n $ stickers**, and we want to know if two friends **share a subset of all $ n $ stickers** in such a way that their **combined** set has **every sticker**.

So, two friends can be **wholesome** if the union of their collections is **full (i.e., includes all $ n $ stickers)**.

So, define the **graph** where each **vertex is a friend**, and an **edge exists between two friends** if and only if their **combined collection contains all $ n $ stickers**, i.e., **they are wholesome**.

We are trying to **color the vertices** (assign them to different tables) such that **no two adjacent vertices (friends who are wholesome) are at the same table**.

This is a **graph coloring** problem, where the **graph is the union of edges between wholesome pairs**, and the **color** represents the **table number**.

Thus, the **minimum number of colors needed** is **the chromatic number of this graph**.

We are told **Marco wants to ensure that no two wholesome friends are at the same table**, hence the **chromatic number** needs to be **at most $ n $**, or in other words, **no more than $ n $ tables are needed** (to be safe).

But how do we know that **$ n $ tables are **sufficient**?

Let’s **look at the case when each friend has exactly 1 sticker**, or when the friends have **very few** in their collection.

Suppose each friend only has **1 sticker**, then their **combined collection is still only 1 sticker**, and hence they are **not wholesome**—they only **collect one**.

In that case, **no two friends are wholesome**, and **only one table is enough**.

But more generally, **if there exists any two friends whose combined collection contains all $ n $ stickers**, they are **wholesome**, and hence **must be assigned to different tables**.

So the **edges** in this graph represent these **wholesome pairs**, and we want to **color the vertices such that no two connected (wholesome) vertices are at the same table**.

This is a **clique graph coloring problem**, specifically, if the **whole set is a complete graph**, then we need to assign each **node to a distinct color**, or at minimum, **one color per connected pair**.

So now, suppose that the **worst-case** is when the **graph is complete**, that is, **every pair of friends is wholesome**.

Then this becomes a **complete graph** of $ n $ vertices, and in such a graph the **chromatic number is exactly $ n $**, since **every vertex must be given a different color**.

Thus, **in the worst case**, the **chromatic number of the graph is $ n $**.

Thus, **Marco may need to reserve $ n $ different tables**, in the **worst-case**.

Hence, **part (i) is verified**:

> **Marco might need to reserve at least $ n $ different tables**.

---

### **(ii) Show that $ n $ tables will always be enough for Marco to achieve his goal.**

We again use **graph coloring** and **the Pigeonhole Principle**.

Given that **no pair of friends is allowed to be on the same table if their combined collection is a full set**, i.e., if their **union has all $ n $ stickers**.

We want to show **$ n $ tables are always enough** to **avoid any wholesome pairs on the same table**.

Again, the key idea is: **if the **graph is such that no two nodes are connected by an edge** (i.e., no two friends are wholesome), then the chromatic number is 1. That is, they can all go to
